  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nThe front that brought yesterday's rainfall continues to move\neast of the region. Higher clouds remain over potions of north-\ncentral Indiana, but will gradually move east through the \nevening. There is a narrow band of convergence that extends \nfrom southern Lake Michigan down to Gibson City, Illinois \nresulting in mid-level cumulus clouds. Clearer skies extend \nnorthwest of the area as today marks the first of several with \ndrier conditions developing.\n\nWinds remain out of the northeast around 10 mph, and are\nexpected to diminish after sunset. With clearer skies, light\nwinds, and cooling temperatures, there is a chance for patchy \nshallow fog for areas away from the Chicago Metro. Widespread \ndense fog is not expected, but satellite trends will need to be \nmonitored overnight. Otherwise, the northerly flow will advect \nin a cooler air mass knocking temperatures down into the 40s \ntomorrow morning; though it would not be surprising if a few \nisolated rural spots touched the upper 30s.\n\nSeasonable temperatures look to set-up through the week next \nweek with high temperatures in the 60s and low 70s and overnight\nlows in the 40s and 50s. Ensemble models are showing some \nconsistency with a couple of fronts passing through the western\nGreat Lakes: one on Sunday, the other in the middle of the \nweek. It is possible that an isolated light rain or sprinkle \ndevelops from it, but as most models favor this remaining in \nWisconsin as it passes over, it may only be the northernmost\ntier of Illinois counties south of the Wisconsin state line \nthat has the best (albeit low) chance for some rain drops. \nOtherwise, the extended forecast keeps us precipitation free.\n\nDK",
